WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:02.640
- [Narrator] China has held the mantle

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:04.160
of the world's biggest polluter

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:06.030
for more than a decade.

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:07.850
It emits the most greenhouse gases

00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:11.130
and uses more coal than the
rest of the world combined.

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:11.970
But in September,

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:14.360
it made the biggest climate
commitment ever made

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:15.559
by any nation.

00:00:15.559 --> 00:00:19.476
(speaking in foreign language)

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:30.620
- [Narrator] China's pledge is expected

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:32.330
to not only improve lifestyle

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:35.180
and air quality for
urbanites tired of smog,

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:37.400
it could also boost the
clean energy business

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.710
and bring hundreds of
millions of electric vehicles

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:41.120
on the road.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:43.310
After President Xi Jinping's announcement,

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:46.263
stocks of Chinese renewable
energy giants soared.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:48.940
There are questions about whether China

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:50.803
can really achieve its climate goal.

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:54.110
But as Beijing pushes ahead with its plan,

00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:55.450
the growing green superpower

00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:57.843
could affect the US and
the rest of the world.

00:00:59.724 --> 00:01:01.470
(lively music)

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:04.020
China's green energy leap won't be cheap.

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:05.420
Over the next 30 years,

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:07.240
Beijing and clean energy companies

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:11.000
will need to spend anywhere
between 5 to $20 trillion.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.840
- So we don't know
exactly how much Beijing

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:13.967
is going to spend.

00:01:13.967 --> 00:01:15.290
Although, it's already spending a lot.

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:17.523
We do know that Beijing's very good

00:01:17.523 --> 00:01:21.273
at stimulating the creation
of very large industries.

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:24.700
- [Narrator] The first step is
ramping up renewable energy.

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:25.990
And that's not impossible

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:27.820
because China already has a lot

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:29.640
of solar parks and wind farms,

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.810
more than any other country.

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:33.060
But China needs a lot more.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:36.710
90% of the country's
energy has to be renewable

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:40.003
or nuclear by 2050 to
achieve net-zero emissions.

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:42.730
In past years, renewable capacity

00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:45.433
has boomed in large part
because of subsidies

00:01:45.433 --> 00:01:47.650
and these companies haven't had to compete

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:49.690
in the global marketplace.

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:52.410
In a strategy shift, Beijing
wants to roll back some

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:54.660
of that direct support for companies.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:56.820
- They kinda slowly dial down the support

00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:01.020
and only the companies that
are actually on quality

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:02.590
and are able to innovate

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:05.336
will be able to survive that.

00:02:05.336 --> 00:02:07.270
(upbeat music)

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:09.343
- [Narrator] Having renewable
energy on tap provides

00:02:09.343 --> 00:02:12.325
a clean source of power
for its electric vehicles.

00:02:12.325 --> 00:02:14.750
China already has the most in the world

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:17.723
and wants 300 million on the road by 2050.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:20.950
- [Nathaniel] China has the
resources to do these kind

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:23.940
of top-down government pushes.

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:26.470
They've just mandated
that a certain percentage

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:28.610
of all government-owned fleets,

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:30.210
like a certain amount of the buses,

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:32.280
a certain amount of the corporate fleets

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:35.070
or the government have to be EVs.

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:38.140
And so that kind of
creates a baseline kind

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:39.763
of structural-level demand.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:42.450
- [Narrator] But Beijing
has also cut subsidies

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:45.070
for people in the market
to buy electric vehicles,

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:47.510
as well as for local manufacturers.

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:50.020
As a result, companies like BYD and Bike

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:51.960
have seen their sales slump,

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:54.020
while Tesla has been able to speed ahead

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:56.500
to become the market leader in China.

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:58.950
But still, Beijing is
keeping foreign automakers

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:02.910
in check by requiring them to
use Chinese-made batteries.

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:05.400
This kind of policy has
helped China get a good grip

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.210
on the supply chain for
lithium ion batteries,

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:11.290
which accounts for more than
half of global production.

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:13.810
But that advantage could slip.

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:16.950
- There's a lot of lithium
out there in the world.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:19.520
If lithium prices start to go up,

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:21.740
I think there'll be a
lot of capital coming

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:23.070
from a lot of different places

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:25.163
to develop those deposits.

00:03:25.163 --> 00:03:27.180
I think it would be quite hard for China

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:29.330
to kind of dominate
the control of lithium.

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:33.100
- [Narrator] Renewables
can only have an impact

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:35.340
if China moves away from coal

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:38.573
but it won't be easy to
switch off these plants.

00:03:38.573 --> 00:03:39.640
- There's a lot of banks that lent

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:40.970
to those coal plants.

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:42.750
That's potentially a financial problem.

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:44.900
There's still a fair
amount of people working

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:46.300
in the coal industry.

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:48.650
So there's gonna be a
lot of vested interests

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:53.650
in keeping coal alive in some
form or another in China.

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:56.180
- [Narrator] Enter carbon
capture technology,

00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:59.250
which would allow some
coal plants to stay online.

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:01.980
The technology would remove
CO2 from the atmosphere

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:04.540
so it can be stored or repurposed.

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:07.110
Many of these techniques
have been around for decades

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:09.380
but it's still incredibly expensive,

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:13.011
costing about $43 to
capture one ton of CO2.

00:04:13.011 --> 00:04:16.390
That means for a country
of 1.4 billion people,

00:04:16.390 --> 00:04:18.210
where each person emits seven tons

00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:20.140
of carbon dioxide every year,

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:22.800
the cost would be astronomical.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:25.230
So Beijing will have to
make polluters responsible

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:27.090
for their own carbon emissions

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:29.060
by making them pay for it.

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:30.875
- They're also gonna have
to create an inventive

00:04:30.875 --> 00:04:33.570
to make it more expensive

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:37.110
to produce emissions by burning coal

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:39.250
or other forms of fossil fuels.

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:42.741
And so a real carbon
trading program in China,

00:04:42.741 --> 00:04:45.390
that would be a huge
incentive for coal producers

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:48.500
to invest in carbon capture technology.

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:50.870
- [Narrator] In October, Chinese
officials announced plans

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:52.870
to expand its emissions trading program

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:54.600
in the next five years.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:56.660
While the current pilot
schemes cover sectors

00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:57.910
like steel and cement,

00:04:57.910 --> 00:05:00.550
the nationwide one would
bring in heavy emitters,

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:02.093
like the aviation industry.

00:05:04.730 --> 00:05:07.071
China's 2060 goal could
have ripple effects.

00:05:07.071 --> 00:05:09.210
Its large-scale investments

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:10.710
could further drive down the costs

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:12.820
of clean tech around the world.

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:16.560
The same way solar panel prices
have plunged more than 80%,

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:19.720
in part because of the
dominance of Chinese firms.

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:21.230
But China isn't alone in thinking

00:05:21.230 --> 00:05:23.310
about this energy transition.

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:25.290
Its relatively smaller neighbors Japan

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:29.340
and South Korea set a 2050
deadline for carbon neutrality.

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:30.620
And some European players

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:32.753
are investing heavily in renewables.

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:36.330
While Trump has signaled that
he sees an energy transition

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:38.319
as a threat to the oil and gas industry,

00:05:38.319 --> 00:05:42.950
Joe Biden has proposed a $2
trillion climate change plan.

00:05:42.950 --> 00:05:44.450
These competitors and countries

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:47.043
could also grow
increasingly wary of China.

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:51.140
- There'll be a worry
both in terms of subsidies

00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:53.170
and China producing things below cost,

00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:55.570
then also being too dependent on China

00:05:55.570 --> 00:05:57.161
for their energy supply.

00:05:57.161 --> 00:05:59.744
(lively music)

